My only change is to `order_processing_workflow.cpp`. `test_005.cpp`/`validate_005.py` exercise `checkout_service.h` and `subscription_billing_job.h`, completely unrelated files — that failure is pre-existing and out of scope for this fix.

## Summary

**Bug:** In `src/workflow/order_processing_workflow.cpp`, `process()` set the order status to `PAID` *before* calling `payment_.charge(...)`. If the charge failed, it was rolled back to `FAILED`, but the order was briefly (and in some read paths, persistently observable as) `PAID` despite no successful authorization — matching the audit finding.

**Fix:** Reordered so `PAID` is only set after `payment_.charge(...)` returns `true`:

```cpp
double total_amount = order.quantity * order.price_per_item;
if (!payment_.charge(order.user_id, total_amount, Currency::USD)) {
    inventory_.release(order.item_id, order.quantity);
    repo_.update_status(order.id, model::OrderStatus::FAILED);
    return false;
}

repo_.update_status(order.id, model::OrderStatus::PAID);
```

Inventory reserve/release flow is untouched: reservation still happens up front, and release-on-payment-failure still fires before marking the order `FAILED`.

Verified with `validate_006.py`, which directly asserts no intermediate `PAID` status update is ever recorded for a failed-payment order — it now passes (`OK`). `validate_005.py` fails, but on unrelated files (`checkout_service`/`subscription_billing_job`) not touched by this change — pre-existing and out of scope.
